{
  "q0": "Industrial Designer described the working design: pressing a button completes a circuit that generates a specific Morse code for that button, the transistor amplifies the signal, and an LED translates it into infrared light that the TV sensor detects and reacts to appropriately.",
  "q1": "The components discussed for remote construction were: case (hard plastic, strong), color components, integrated circuit (highly sophisticated, temperature resistant with timer and alarm), resistor, capacitor, diode, transistor, resonator, battery (with discussion about rechargeable options), and circuit board.",
  "q2": "The meeting concluded with the next meeting scheduled for 30 minutes. Individual actions include: Industrial Designer to develop look and feel design, User Interface Designer to create user interface design, and Marketing to evaluate the product. Jana and Francine were to collaborate on a prototype using modeling clay.",
  "q3": "The interface design would be a trendy, compact, and strong remote with push buttons as the primary interface elements. Speech recognition was considered as a potential feature but would require further research on cost and reliability before implementation."
}

{
  "q0": "The model showed mixed results across different language conditions: out of six cases, a couple stayed about the same, three improved, and one got worse. They had an average of 49% improvement on other tasks, with the best system achieving about 54% reduction in word error rate. The model reduced error rates from a quarter to half of the original, depending on the language and conditions.",
  "q1": "PhD C asked about experiments using data from multiple languages to train neural networks. PhD A explained that they tried this approach but it didn't work as expected, suggesting there might be an issue with how they're handling the labels rather than a bug. They noted that hand-labeled data worked best but it's difficult to find for other languages.",
  "q2": "The professor thought it was important to look at what's really going on with the data and see if there's a way to combine the best ideas from different approaches while keeping resource usage within constraints for mobile devices. He noted that while they had made significant improvements (30-60% error rate reduction), the results were still too poor for practical use, and the realistic nature of the Aurora data made it particularly challenging.",
  "q3": "The professor discussed the need to understand what's causing the performance issues and find a way to combine the best ideas from different approaches without increasing resource requirements too much (critical for mobile devices). He noted that while they had made significant improvements (30-60% error rate reduction), the results were still too poor for practical use, and the realistic nature of the Aurora data (with real noise and reflections) made it particularly challenging to achieve good performance."
}
